mt76: mt76x02: acknowledge every raised interrupt source

The handler masks the interrupt source register with irqmask before writing
it back, so a source the driver does not subscribe to is never acknowledged.
The device keeps MT_INT_TXDELAYINT raised on an MT7662 here, it is not part
of irqmask, and it was therefore left asserted forever.

Where the interrupt is edge triggered at the host that costs nothing, which
is presumably why this went unnoticed.  On a shared legacy INTx line it is
fatal: the pin stays asserted, the host re-enters the handler as fast as it
can run it, and the CPU never gets to do anything else.  Observed on an
EcoNet EN751221 board, where the machine goes unresponsive with no panic and
no log -- the interrupt storm leaves no room for the watchdog threads, the
timers, or even the console to run.

Write the raised sources back first and narrow to irqmask afterwards, so
nothing is left pending.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
--- a/mt76x02_mmio.c
+++ b/mt76x02_mmio.c
@@ -261,8 +261,17 @@
 	u32 intr, mask;
 
 	intr = mt76_rr(dev, MT_INT_SOURCE_CSR);
-	intr &= dev->mt76.mmio.irqmask;
+	/*
+	 * Acknowledge everything the device raised, then pick out what we
+	 * handle. Masking first leaves any source we do not subscribe to
+	 * asserted for good: the chip keeps raising TXDELAYINT here, it never
+	 * appears in irqmask, and so it was never written back. On a shared
+	 * INTx line that is enough to keep the pin low and have the host
+	 * re-enter this handler forever, which starves everything else on the
+	 * CPU -- no timers, no threads, and nothing in the log to say why.
+	 */
 	mt76_wr(dev, MT_INT_SOURCE_CSR, intr);
+	intr &= dev->mt76.mmio.irqmask;
 
 	if (!test_bit(MT76_STATE_INITIALIZED, &dev->mphy.state))
 		return IRQ_NONE;
